   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to decisions about routine\nmedical treatment for hospice patients without a surrogate decision maker",
    "basePrintNo": "S6356",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6356-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2994-g, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6356\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\ndecisions about routine medical treatment for hospice patients without a\nsurrogate decision maker\n\n \nPURPOSE OF GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo clarify that decisions about routine care for hospice patients with-\nout a surrogate decision maker do not require prior review by a n Ethics\nReview Committee, and are governed by the provisions in the Family\nHealth Care Decisions Act.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends PHL § 2994-g(5-a), which requires review by an Ethics\nReview Committee of health care decisions for hospice patients who do\nnot have a surrogate decision maker, to provide that such requirement\nshall not apply to decisions about routine medical treatment, and that\nsuch decisions shall be governed by PHL § 2994-g(3).\n\nSection 2 provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Family Health Care Decisions Act (FHCDA), in PHL § 2994-(g), estab-\nlishes a process to obtain health care decisions for patients who lack\ncapacity and do not have a health care agent or surrogate decision maker\n- so called \"isolated patients.\" Under that section, the attending\nphysician may order \"routine medical treatment,\" while more extensive\nprocesses and standards apply to decisions about \"major medical treat-\nment\" and \"decisions to withdraw or withhold life-sustaining treatment.\"\n\nThat section defines \"routine medical treatment\" as: any treatment,\nservice, or procedure to diagnose or treat an individual's physical or\nmental condition, such as the administration of medication, the\nextraction of bodily fluids for analysis, or dental care performed.\nwith a local anesthetic, for which health care providers ordinarily do\nnot seek specific consent from the patient or authorized representative.\n\nIn 2016, the FHCDA was amended to address decisions regarding hospice\ncare, including enrollment in hospice, for incapable isolated patients.\nThe process requires, among other steps, review by a facility-based\nEthics Review Committee (ERC). Once admitted to hospice, changes in the\nisolated hospice patient's plan of care also require ERC review.\n\nCommentators have noted that changes in the plan of care involving even\nroutine medical treatment, such as drawing blood, would seem to require\nERC review (Decisions Regarding Hospice Care for. Isolated Patients: A\nGuide to the 2015 Amendment of the Family Health Care Decisions Act,\nNYSBA Health Law Journal (Winter 2016) Such requirement is far more\ncumbersome than is needed for decisions about minor treatments, and\nwould impede the prompt provision of medically necessary care. The bill\nclarifies that the general FHCDA provisions relating to decisions about\nroutine medical treatment apply to isolated hospice patients as well as\nother patients.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe act will take effect immediately.\n",
